[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233628 :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "a903e74e-e530-53d5-8ad7-3ef2b34613b3" :question/math-academy-id "q-211305" :question/problem "![](images/d8/d8d23cf36bc9fb526c0e6d041b72e6b378638f662b67aae63531c66b4a8cd6c5.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows the local minimum and maximum of the function $y=f(x).$ What is the local maximum of $y=3f(\\frac{x}{2})?$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that the graph of $y=3f(\\frac{x}{2})$ can be obtained from the graph of $y=f(x)$ by \n\n- stretching it by a scale factor of $2$ parallel to the $x$-axis, and then\n\n- stretching it by a scale factor of $3$ parallel to the $y$-axis.\n\nSince these transformations do not flip the graph across the $x$-axis, the combined transformation maps the local maximum of $y=f(x)$ to the local maximum of $y=3f(\\frac{x}{2}).$ So, we need to find the image of $(-2,-1),$ the local maximum of $y=f(x),$ under the transformation. \n\nTo plot $y=3f(\\frac{x}{2}),$ we apply the following steps: \n\n- First, we take the graph $y=f(x)$ that passes through $(-2,-1).$\n\n- Then, we  stretch the graph  along the $x$-axis by a scale factor of $2$ and get the graph of $y=f(\\frac{x}{2}).$ After this transformation, the point $(-2,-1)$ will be mapped to the point\n\n$$\n(2\\cdot (-2),-1)=(-4,-1).\n$$\n\n- Finally, we stretch the result  along the $y$-axis by a scale factor of $3$ and get the graph of $y=3f(\\frac{x}{2}).$ After this transformation, the point $(-4,-1)$ will be mapped to the point\n\n$$\n(-4,3\\cdot (-1))=(-4,-3).\n$$\n\nTherefore, the local maximum of $y=3f(\\frac{x}{2})$ is $(-4,-3).$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233643 :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "6b6e2582-e861-5aac-850f-2f62d29905a5" :question/math-academy-id "q-2251" :question/problem "Find the antiderivative of $3x+6.$" :question/worked-solution "Using the sum and constant factor rules, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}\\int 3x+6\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int 3x\\,\\text{d}x+\\int 6\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =3\\int x\\,\\text{d}x+6\\int 1\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =3(\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2})+6(x)+C \\\\  & =\\frac{3{x}^{2}}{2}+6x+C.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233644 :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "8f7dc381-ff4a-5ab2-9c80-1b180b43d77b" :question/math-academy-id "q-2252" :question/problem "Find the antiderivative of $2{x}^{2}-x+\\frac{1}{2}.$" :question/worked-solution "Using the sum and constant factor rules, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}\\int (2{x}^{2}-x+\\frac{1}{2})\\,\\text{d}x & =2\\int {x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x-\\int x\\,\\text{d}x+\\int \\frac{1}{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2(\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3})-(\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2})+(\\frac{x}{2})+C \\\\  & =\\frac{2{x}^{3}}{3}-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}+\\frac{x}{2}+C.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233645 :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "7217304d-4e39-5138-b636-a65ba43dac92" :question/math-academy-id "q-2288" :question/problem "Given that $\\operatorname{log}(y)-{x}^{2}=3,$ calculate $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we differentiate both sides of the equation with respect to $x,$ assuming that $y=y(x).$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{log}(y)-{x}^{2} & =3 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{log}(y)-{x}^{2}) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(3) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{log}(y))-\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{2}) & =0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{y\\operatorname{ln}(10)}(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x})-2x & =0\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow we solve the equation for $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{y\\cdot \\operatorname{ln}(10)}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =2x \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =(2x)(y\\operatorname{ln}(10)) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =2\\operatorname{ln}(10)\\,xy\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233649 :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "4f796b6e-f558-59cc-b712-614645986a98" :question/math-academy-id "q-22963" :question/problem "What is the angle of $\\frac{5\\pi }{9}$ radians in degrees?" :question/worked-solution "To convert the measure of an angle in radians to an equivalent measure in degrees, we multiply the measure in radians by $\\frac{{180}^{∘}}{\\pi }.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\frac{5\\pi }{9})\\cdot (\\frac{{180}^{∘}}{\\pi }) & =\\frac{5\\cdot {180}^{∘}\\cdot \\pi }{9\\cdot \\pi } \\\\  & =\\frac{5\\cdot {20}^{∘}\\cdot \\pi }{\\pi } \\\\  & =\\frac{{100}^{∘}\\cancel{\\pi }}{\\cancel{\\pi }} \\\\  & ={100}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $\\frac{5\\pi }{9}$ is equivalent to ${100}^{∘}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233651 :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "f8ed1969-ee0f-5dc8-86e8-8af537738cdd" :question/math-academy-id "q-23197" :question/problem "Given that ${log}_{3}x=\\frac{{log}_{9}x}{k},$ what is the value of $k?$" :question/worked-solution "We use the change of base formula:\n\n$$\n{log}_{a}X=\\frac{{log}_{b}X}{{log}_{b}a}\n$$\n\nWe want to change from base $a=3$ to base $b=9.$ So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{log}_{3}x & =\\frac{{log}_{9}x}{{log}_{9}3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRewriting the expression in the denominator using exponents, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{log}_{9}x}{{log}_{9}3} & =\\frac{{log}_{9}x}{{log}_{9}({9}^{1/2})} \\\\  & =\\frac{{log}_{9}x}{(\\frac{1}{2})}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n{log}_{3}x=\\frac{{log}_{9}x}{(\\frac{1}{2})}.\n$$\n\nHence, $k=\\frac{1}{2}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233652 :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "931ba62b-019a-5f13-98b6-b18422089fa3" :question/math-academy-id "q-23381" :question/problem "$\\operatorname{cos}(\\frac{4\\pi }{3})=$" :question/worked-solution "Let's remind ourselves of the special trigonometric ratios, as given by the unit circle.\n\n![](images/db/dbf36192ac332dc49ed75278838e989a37ac7d888311be1b8b059a7b59667aa6.png)\n\nFrom the unit circle, we see that the angle $\\frac{4\\pi }{3}$ corresponds to the point $(-\\frac{1}{2},-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}).$\n\nSince the cosine of the angle corresponds to the $x$-coordinate, we have $\\operatorname{cos}(\\frac{4\\pi }{3})=-\\frac{1}{2}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233662 :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "8304b514-754e-54d2-b053-31a4e39c33dd" :question/math-academy-id "q-2400" :question/problem "Find ${y}^{'}$ for $y=\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}({x}^{3})}{{x}^{3}+2}.$" :question/worked-solution "We use the quotient rule with the numerator $u(x)=\\operatorname{ln}({x}^{3})$ and the denominator $v(x)={x}^{3}+2.$ \n\nDifferentiating $u(x)$ and $v(x),$ we have\n\n$$\n{u}^{'}(x)=\\frac{3}{x},\\,{v}^{'}(x)=3{x}^{2}.\n$$\n\nNote that we used the chain rule to differentiate $u.$\n\nNow, applying the quotient rule and simplifying, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}{y}^{'}=\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{{u}^{'}(x)\\cdot v(x)-u(x)\\cdot {v}^{'}(x)}{[v(x)]^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{3}{x})({x}^{3}+2)-(\\operatorname{ln}({x}^{3}))(3{x}^{2})}{({x}^{3}+2)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{3({x}^{3}+2)-3{x}^{3}\\operatorname{ln}({x}^{3})}{x({x}^{3}+2)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{3{x}^{3}-3{x}^{3}\\operatorname{ln}({x}^{3})+6}{x({x}^{3}+2)^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233663 :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "f287ce9d-53a8-5db5-b95e-41eedc3481e1" :question/math-academy-id "q-24084" :question/problem "Simplify the expression $\\frac{\\operatorname{sec}\\theta +\\operatorname{csc}\\theta }{\\operatorname{sin}\\theta +\\operatorname{cos}\\theta }.$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's interpret the fraction as a division:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\operatorname{sec}\\theta +\\operatorname{csc}\\theta }{\\operatorname{sin}\\theta +\\operatorname{cos}\\theta }=(\\operatorname{sec}\\theta +\\operatorname{csc}\\theta )÷(\\operatorname{sin}\\theta +\\operatorname{cos}\\theta )\n$$\n\nThen, we recall the identities $\\operatorname{sec}\\theta =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}\\theta }$ and $\\operatorname{csc}\\theta =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}\\theta },$ and we substitute them into the above expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\operatorname{sec}\\theta +\\operatorname{csc}\\theta )÷(\\operatorname{sin}\\theta +\\operatorname{cos}\\theta ) & =(\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}\\theta }+\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}\\theta })÷(\\operatorname{sin}\\theta +\\operatorname{cos}\\theta ) \\\\  & =(\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}\\theta +\\operatorname{cos}\\theta }{\\operatorname{cos}\\theta \\cdot \\operatorname{sin}\\theta })÷(\\operatorname{sin}\\theta +\\operatorname{cos}\\theta )\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we turn the division into multiplication, and simplify the expression as much as possible:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}\\theta +\\operatorname{cos}\\theta }{\\operatorname{cos}\\theta \\cdot \\operatorname{sin}\\theta })÷(\\operatorname{sin}\\theta +\\operatorname{cos}\\theta ) & =(\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}\\theta +\\operatorname{cos}\\theta }{\\operatorname{cos}\\theta \\cdot \\operatorname{sin}\\theta })\\cdot (\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}\\theta +\\operatorname{cos}\\theta }) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}\\theta +\\operatorname{cos}\\theta }{\\operatorname{cos}\\theta \\cdot \\operatorname{sin}\\theta (\\operatorname{sin}\\theta +\\operatorname{cos}\\theta )} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\cancel{(\\operatorname{sin}\\theta +\\operatorname{cos}\\theta )}}{\\operatorname{cos}\\theta \\cdot \\operatorname{sin}\\theta \\cancel{(\\operatorname{sin}\\theta +\\operatorname{cos}\\theta )}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}\\theta \\cdot \\operatorname{sin}\\theta } \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}\\theta }\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}\\theta } \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sec}\\theta \\operatorname{csc}\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $\\frac{\\operatorname{sec}\\theta +\\operatorname{csc}\\theta }{\\operatorname{sin}\\theta +\\operatorname{cos}\\theta }=\\operatorname{sec}\\theta \\operatorname{csc}\\theta $"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233671 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a7096c7a-c8d9-5113-9f0e-27cb22032898" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186232355}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a7096c7a-c8d9-5113-9f0e-27cb22032898" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186232355} :answer-field/id #uuid "647c4ca4-fef0-5f88-8d58-74407f510be7" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186232354}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "3861808c-8376-539b-a5cb-a658d49c4e99" :question/math-academy-id "q-246824" :question/problem "$\\operatorname{cos}0+\\operatorname{sin}(\\frac{\\pi }{2})=${{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "The angles $0$ and $\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ are quadrantal angles. Let's draw out the quadrantal angles on the unit circle.\n\n![](images/ed/edad8c833f65c54453fe40a326cbf9394c263213934447872b530a8e4fb86de1.png)\n\n- Let's first consider the angle $0.$ From the unit circle, we see that the angle $0$ corresponds to the point $(1,0).$ Since the cosine of the angle corresponds to the $x$-coordinate, we have $\\operatorname{cos}0=1.$\n\n- Now, let's consider the angle $\\frac{\\pi }{2}.$ From the unit circle, we see that the angle $\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ corresponds to the point $(0,1).$ Since the sine of the angle corresponds to the $y$-coordinate, we have $\\operatorname{sin}(\\frac{\\pi }{2})=1.$\n\nFinally, we get\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}0+\\operatorname{sin}(\\frac{\\pi }{2})=1+1=2.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233674 :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "0cf4f6aa-d2fd-561c-8841-ebb1640f046c" :question/math-academy-id "q-24901" :question/problem "$\\operatorname{arcsin}(-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2})=$" :question/worked-solution "First, note that the range of $\\theta =\\operatorname{arcsin}x$ is given by\n\n$$\n-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{2}.\n$$\n\nNow, let's take a look at the unit circle.\n\n![](images/db/dbf36192ac332dc49ed75278838e989a37ac7d888311be1b8b059a7b59667aa6.png)\n\nFrom the values in the unit circle, we see that\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}(\\frac{7\\pi }{4})=-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}.\n$$\n\nHowever, $\\frac{7\\pi }{4}$ does not lie within the range of $\\theta .$ To get an angle that's coterminal with $\\frac{7\\pi }{4}$ that lies within the range of $\\theta ,$ we subtract $2\\pi .$\n\n$$\n\\frac{7\\pi }{4}-2\\pi =-\\frac{\\pi }{4}.\n$$\n\nHence,\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{arcsin}(-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2})=-\\frac{\\pi }{4}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233681 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1a702138-eb7f-57f2-85da-25ca401ae0d1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2C}{\\pi }" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186290742} {:answer/id #uuid "3976265e-a352-578b-a357-2cd0a06c4939" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{C}{4\\pi }" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186290869} {:answer/id #uuid "753117d6-33c7-54de-91ff-a3f4c5f14609" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291132} {:answer/id #uuid "9e5a7c73-e816-5f34-827a-00a560b45606" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "C\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291319} {:answer/id #uuid "c2364d85-a5d6-5def-95a8-67ff25fb3ee8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{C}{2\\pi }" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291465}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c2364d85-a5d6-5def-95a8-67ff25fb3ee8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{C}{2\\pi }" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291465} :answer-field/id #uuid "a135f47c-1bca-5dab-873e-5d9809c3f67c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292247}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "e1c06bae-9b79-5f79-a18c-61e151171196" :question/math-academy-id "q-2545" :question/problem "![](images/f5/f59f65e184ccf4fdf2ae2124543ece45d41afce09d743dec9f17730dd00124d4.png)\n\nThe area $A$ of the circle above is increasing. The circumference and radius of the circle are $C$ and $r$ respectively. \nFind the rate of change of the area of the circle with respect to its circumference." :question/worked-solution "The area of a circle is given by\n\n$$\nA=\\pi {r}^{2},\n$$\n\nwhere $r$ is the radius of the circle.\n\nWe know that $C=2\\pi r.$ so we can express the area $A$ as a function of the circumference $C$ by substituting $r=\\frac{C}{2\\pi }$ in the formula above. We get\n\n$$\nA=\\pi {r}^{2}=\\pi {(\\frac{C}{2\\pi })}^{2}=\\frac{{C}^{2}}{4\\pi }.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the rate of change of $A(C)$ with respect to $C$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{′}(C) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}C}(\\frac{{C}^{2}}{4\\pi }) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4\\pi }\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}C}({C}^{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4\\pi }(2C) \\\\  & =\\frac{C}{2\\pi }.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233694 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "fd4170d8-156b-5d97-99cc-f670270c4411" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186232380}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fd4170d8-156b-5d97-99cc-f670270c4411" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186232380} :answer-field/id #uuid "8f37d65d-44e6-56b2-b42d-4321fa888aec" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186232379}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "a7a046d0-9e17-55be-a829-48218cb514b0" :question/math-academy-id "q-262541" :question/problem "Find the value of $\\operatorname{sec}\\pi +\\operatorname{csc}(\\frac{\\pi }{2}).$\n\n$\\operatorname{sec}\\pi +\\operatorname{csc}(\\frac{\\pi }{2})=\\,$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "The angles $\\pi $ and $\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ are quadrantal angles. Let's draw out the quadrantal angles on the unit circle.\n\n![](images/ed/edad8c833f65c54453fe40a326cbf9394c263213934447872b530a8e4fb86de1.png)\n\n- Let's first consider the angle $\\pi .$ From the unit circle, we see that the angle $\\pi $ corresponds to the point $(-1,0).$  Since the cosine of the angle corresponds to the $x$-coordinate, we have $\\operatorname{cos}\\pi =-1.$ Therefore, using the fact that $\\operatorname{sec}\\theta =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}\\theta },$ we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sec}\\pi =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}\\pi }=\\frac{1}{-1}=-1.\n$$\n\n- Now, let's consider the angle $\\frac{\\pi }{2}.$ From the unit circle, we see that the angle $\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ corresponds to the point $(0,1).$  Since the sine of the angle corresponds to the $y$-coordinate, we have $\\operatorname{sin}(\\frac{\\pi }{2})=1.$ Therefore, using the fact that $\\operatorname{csc}\\theta =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}\\theta },$ we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{csc}(\\frac{\\pi }{2})=\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}(\\frac{\\pi }{2})}=\\frac{1}{1}=1.\n$$\n\nFinally, we get\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sec}\\pi +\\operatorname{csc}(\\frac{\\pi }{2})=-1+1=0.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233698 :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "5038bcdd-b5fa-523b-bcb5-164cac5d163b" :question/math-academy-id "q-26531" :question/problem "If ${log}_{3}(6-2x)=4{log}_{3}(\\sqrt{3})$, then what is the value of $x?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we use the power rule to simplify the right-hand side. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{log}_{3}(6-2x) & =4{log}_{3}(\\sqrt{3}) \\\\ {log}_{3}(6-2x) & ={log}_{3}({(\\sqrt{3})}^{4}) \\\\ {log}_{3}(6-2x) & ={log}_{3}(9).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the logarithms on both sides of the equation have the same base, we can equate the arguments. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{log}_{3}(6-2x) & ={log}_{3}(9) \\\\ 6-2x & =9 \\\\ 2x & =-3 \\\\ x & =-\\frac{3}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's now check for extraneous solutions by substituting back into the original equation: \n\n- Substituting $x=-\\frac{3}{2}$ back into the original equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{log}_{3}(6-2x) & ={log}_{3}(6-2(-\\frac{3}{2})) \\\\  & ={log}_{3}(6+3) \\\\  & ={log}_{3}(9) \\\\  & ={log}_{3}(\\sqrt{3})^{4} \\\\  & =4{log}_{3}(\\sqrt{3}).\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $x=-\\frac{3}{2}$ is a valid solution."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233710 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b353bd1b-1c5e-529b-86dd-9fd5edfe9c4c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2.16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186232406}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b353bd1b-1c5e-529b-86dd-9fd5edfe9c4c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2.16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186232406} :answer-field/id #uuid "8a8a3f0f-e235-523e-966e-179bafec3a3b" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186232405}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "2ca890be-c883-5ea3-81cb-a98892d9c262" :question/math-academy-id "q-275157" :question/problem "Find, **in radians**, the argument of $z=-4+6\\text{i}.$ Round your final answer to **two decimal places**.\n\n**Hint**: The argument of a complex number is in the range $[0,2\\pi ).$ \n\n$\\operatorname{arg}⁡(z)≈$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Let's plot $z$ in the complex plane.\n\n![](images/32/325444dbc509efd27f6c5bf16c751b137532e2566a9dfaa69f92d9ce16bac51b.png)\n\nThe complex number $z$ lies in the second quadrant of the complex plane. Hence $\\frac{\\pi }{2}<\\operatorname{arg}⁡(z)<\\pi .$ \n\nIf $\\alpha $ is the angle that $z$ makes with the negative real axis, then\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\alpha  & =\\frac{6}{4} \\\\ \\alpha  & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{6}{4}) \\\\  & =0.982\\text{ }793…\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\theta =\\operatorname{arg}⁡(z)=\\pi -0.982\\text{ }793…≈2.16,\n$$\n\nrounded to $2$ decimal places."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233711 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "aa713718-4023-5280-813d-3aa91128a3be" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5.62" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186232408}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "aa713718-4023-5280-813d-3aa91128a3be" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5.62" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186232408} :answer-field/id #uuid "2bdc18ae-186c-5350-9538-a2d1a6ea5f2a" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186232407}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "0c91f4f8-2fbe-5963-a53b-2aead62a5f7f" :question/math-academy-id "q-275159" :question/problem "Find, **in radians**, the argument of $z=9-7\\text{i}.$ Round your final answer to **two decimal places**.\n\n**Hint**: The argument of a complex number is in the range $[0,2\\pi ).$ \n\n$\\operatorname{arg}⁡(z)≈$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Let's plot $z$ in the complex plane.\n\n![](images/b6/b6ec4332891a4c7684be8de2ca94bf24a1d9abd5170d9fe38ad3a1346afae56a.png)\n\nThe complex number $z$ lies in the fourth quadrant of the complex plane. Hence $\\frac{3\\pi }{2}<\\operatorname{arg}⁡(z)<2\\pi .$ \n\nIf $\\alpha $ is the angle that $z$ makes with the positive real axis, then\n\n$$\n\\alpha =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{7}{9})=0.661\\,043…\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\theta =\\operatorname{arg}⁡(z)=2\\pi -0.661\\,043≈5.62,\n$$\n\nrounded to $2$ decimal places."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233712 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "046339ca-d635-57b1-a4b9-a7619ba24dea" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512111} {:answer/id #uuid "1eededde-27c4-5ab6-921a-69c922b3ddd0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512163} {:answer/id #uuid "4846d2a5-0437-5027-a18c-e8704132c534" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512253} {:answer/id #uuid "8dc266e6-456a-5c58-bbb2-5385368780aa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512431} {:answer/id #uuid "ec722ee2-a7c4-5b69-bb9e-dbec3b88f301" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512672}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ec722ee2-a7c4-5b69-bb9e-dbec3b88f301" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512672} :answer-field/id #uuid "da30e9ce-0a25-5add-9fa0-a7b33a5bb2d1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512843}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "1ad05a96-8f5a-585b-894f-27d371577458" :question/math-academy-id "q-27541" :question/problem "![](images/41/41875c5ab1216b78760c1c2dddec6ffc31bfd57d2da27f4797b1190dbf4af709.png)\n\nThe graph above shows the curve $y=k{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{x}+c.$ Find the value of $k-c.$" :question/worked-solution "The horizontal asymptote of the curve $y=k{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{x}+c$ is $y=c.$ In the graph above, the horizontal asymptote is $y=-3.$ Hence, $c=-3.$\n\nWe can solve for $k$ by finding a point on the graph and substituting for $x$ and $y.$ The labeled point on the graph is $(0,1),$ so we substitute $x=0$ and $y=1$ into the function and solve for $k:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}k{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{x}+c & =y \\\\ k{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{x}-3 & =y \\\\ k{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{0}-3 & =1 \\\\ k-3 & =1 \\\\ k & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nk-c=4-(-3)=7.\n$$\n\nThe equation of the curve is $y=4{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{x}-3.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233731 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "73e50f8a-7da5-506f-8d57-7bcc1d8f207c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{781}{64}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186232493}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "73e50f8a-7da5-506f-8d57-7bcc1d8f207c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{781}{64}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186232493} :answer-field/id #uuid "2af429bd-0da0-5fd5-8057-0936799bb009" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186232492}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "35410571-f882-5cd6-b192-eea7f8f346ac" :question/math-academy-id "q-280142" :question/problem "Evaluate the **exact value** of the following series:\n\n${\\sum }_{n=1}^{5}4{(\\frac{3}{4})}^{n-1}=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Since the given series is in the form ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{N}{a}_{1}{r}^{n-1},$ it is a geometric series. \n\nTo determine the sum, we use the formula for the sum ${S}_{N}$ of a geometric series.\n\n$$\n{S}_{N}={a}_{1}\\frac{(1-{r}^{N})}{1-r}\n$$\n\nIn this case, ${a}_{1}=4,$ $r=\\frac{3}{4}$ and $N=5.$ If we substitute these values into the formula for ${S}_{N}$, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{n=1}^{5}4{(\\frac{3}{4})}^{n-1} & =4\\cdot \\frac{(1-{(\\frac{3}{4})}^{5})}{1-\\frac{3}{4}} \\\\  & =4\\cdot \\frac{(1-\\frac{243}{1024})}{\\frac{1}{4}} \\\\  & =4\\cdot \\frac{\\frac{781}{1024}}{\\frac{1}{4}} \\\\  & =4\\cdot \\frac{3124}{1024} \\\\  & =\\frac{781}{64}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233733 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f054a037-6b80-5315-b68a-ba30675c8a02" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1200}{343}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186232497}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f054a037-6b80-5315-b68a-ba30675c8a02" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1200}{343}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186232497} :answer-field/id #uuid "28138931-dbd9-5fd8-8996-844113417761" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186232496}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "4f81bfb0-626f-5170-9d48-74b4a7a34a86" :question/math-academy-id "q-280244" :question/problem "Evaluate the **exact value** of the following geometric series:\n\n${\\sum }_{n=1}^{4}\\frac{21}{{7}^{n}}=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Notice that we can rewrite the series as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{n=1}^{4}\\frac{21}{{7}^{n}}={\\sum }_{n=1}^{4}21\\cdot {(\\frac{1}{7})}^{n}\n$$\n\nSince the above series is in the form ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{N}a{r}^{n},$ it is a geometric series. \n\nTo determine the sum, we use the formula for the sum ${S}_{N}$ of a geometric series.\n\n$$\n{S}_{N}={a}_{1}\\frac{(1-{r}^{N})}{1-r}\n$$\n\nIn this case, $r=\\frac{1}{7}$ and $N=4.$ We need to figure out ${a}_{1}.$\n\nThe formula for the $n$th term of the series is\n\n$$\n{a}_{n}=21\\cdot {(\\frac{1}{7})}^{n},\n$$\n\nand therefore the first term of the series is\n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=21\\cdot {(\\frac{1}{7})}^{1}=3.\n$$\n\nIf we substitute ${a}_{1}=3,r=\\frac{1}{7}$ and $N=4$ into the formula for ${S}_{N}$, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{n=1}^{4}21\\cdot {(\\frac{1}{7})}^{n} & =3\\cdot \\frac{[1-{(\\frac{1}{7})}^{4}]}{(1-\\frac{1}{7})} \\\\  & =3\\cdot \\frac{(1-\\frac{1}{2401})}{\\frac{6}{7}} \\\\  & =3\\cdot \\frac{7}{6}\\cdot \\frac{2400}{2401} \\\\  & =\\frac{1200}{343}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233734 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c065f506-19bc-572f-8984-515d609a71bc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{259}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186232499}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c065f506-19bc-572f-8984-515d609a71bc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{259}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186232499} :answer-field/id #uuid "983ef0c4-a44d-5516-9dce-a08dfd32b508" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186232498}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "4ac5a536-3bf2-576d-8372-398379aeac57" :question/math-academy-id "q-280253" :question/problem "Evaluate the **exact value** of the following geometric series:\n\n${\\sum }_{n=1}^{4}54\\cdot {(\\frac{1}{6})}^{n-1}=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Notice that we can rewrite the series as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{n=1}^{4}54\\cdot {(\\frac{1}{6})}^{n-1}\n$$\n\nSince the above series is in the form ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{N}a{r}^{n-1}$, it is a geometric series starting at $n=1$, with the exponent matching the standard form for partial sums.\n\nTo determine the sum, we use the formula for the sum ${S}_{N}$ of a geometric series:\n\n$$\n{S}_{N}={a}_{1}\\frac{(1-{r}^{N})}{1-r}\n$$\n\nIn this case, $r=\\frac{1}{6}$, $N=4$, and the first term ${a}_{1}=54.$\n\nSubstituting into the formula gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{n=1}^{4}54\\cdot {(\\frac{1}{6})}^{n-1} & =54\\cdot \\frac{1-{(\\frac{1}{6})}^{4}}{1-\\frac{1}{6}} \\\\  & =54\\cdot \\frac{1295}{1296}\\cdot \\frac{6}{5} \\\\  & =\\frac{259}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233755 :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "0bf78498-fa41-5c5b-8e73-bc357aed8c2f" :question/math-academy-id "q-29825" :question/problem "Find the antiderivative of $3{x}^{2}-\\frac{2}{{x}^{3}}.$" :question/worked-solution "Using the sum and constant factor rules, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int (3{x}^{2}-\\frac{2}{{x}^{3}})\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int 3{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x-\\int 2{x}^{-3}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =3\\int {x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x-2\\int {x}^{-3}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =3(\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3})-2(\\frac{{x}^{-2}}{-2})+C \\\\  & ={x}^{3}+{x}^{-2}+C \\\\  & ={x}^{3}+\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233764 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "177640d9-b92e-5b99-8c1b-c6f815b05f0d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186232526}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "177640d9-b92e-5b99-8c1b-c6f815b05f0d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186232526} :answer-field/id #uuid "1d967f44-5321-551a-8728-687e77eddd7b" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186232525}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "0d2c1dd9-f844-54d2-b778-954be025fd44" :question/math-academy-id "q-302415" :question/problem "Evaluate the following limit:\n\n${lim}_{x\\to \\pi /4}\\operatorname{csc}(2x)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Substituting $x=\\frac{\\pi }{4}$ directly into the limit, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\pi /4}\\operatorname{csc}(2x) & =\\operatorname{csc}(2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{4}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}(\\frac{\\pi }{2})} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{1} \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233765 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "62875253-4bd4-58c8-906e-a1cd0d365347" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186232528}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "62875253-4bd4-58c8-906e-a1cd0d365347" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186232528} :answer-field/id #uuid "37edafc5-2f02-581a-9fe0-3e64cea2958f" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186232527}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "2c628f1c-4b41-52b5-9e0c-67f83e5ffd9e" :question/math-academy-id "q-302430" :question/problem "Evaluate the following limit:\n\n${lim}_{x\\to \\frac{\\pi }{2}}4\\operatorname{cot}(x-\\frac{\\pi }{4})=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Substituting $x=\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ directly into the limit, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\frac{\\pi }{2}}4\\operatorname{cot}(x-\\frac{\\pi }{4}) & =4\\operatorname{cot}(\\frac{\\pi }{2}-\\frac{\\pi }{4}) \\\\  & =4\\operatorname{cot}(\\frac{\\pi }{4}) \\\\  & =4\\cdot \\frac{\\operatorname{cos}(\\frac{\\pi }{4})}{\\operatorname{sin}(\\frac{\\pi }{4})} \\\\  & =4\\cdot \\frac{(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2})}{(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2})} \\\\  & =4\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233781 :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "73219a58-c08c-520e-96a1-4134d001c0d8" :question/math-academy-id "q-31288" :question/problem "Find ${f}^{'}(\\frac{\\pi }{6})$ if $f(x)=-6\\operatorname{cot}x.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we just take the constant out of the derivative and use the result for the derivative of $\\operatorname{cot}x,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}{f}^{'}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(-6\\operatorname{cot}x) \\\\  & =-6(-{csc}^{2}x) \\\\  & =6{csc}^{2}x\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we substitute $x=\\frac{\\pi }{6}$ in the derivative and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{'}(\\frac{\\pi }{6}) & =6{csc}^{2}(\\frac{\\pi }{6}) \\\\  & =6{(2)}^{2} \\\\  & =24.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233783 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/id #uuid "4aac1b00-ccaf-57eb-98c1-277328cf34e0" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186232543}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "cd31dbf5-1a9b-54a3-a636-334695ca85d4" :question/math-academy-id "q-313299" :question/problem "Find the slope $m$ of the tangent line to the curve $y={x}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}(2x)$ at $x=\\frac{\\pi }{4}.$\n\n$m=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "To find the slope at a given point, we have to find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ at that point.\n\nThis question requires the product rule. Let $u(x)={x}^{2}$ and $v(x)=\\operatorname{sin}2x.$ Then\n\n$$\n{u}^{'}(x)=2x,\\,{v}^{'}(x)=2\\operatorname{cos}2x.\n$$\n\nNote that we used the chain rule to differentiate $v.$\n\nNow, applying the product rule and simplifying, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & ={u}^{'}(x)\\cdot v(x)+u(x)\\cdot {v}^{'}(x) \\\\  & =(2x)(\\operatorname{sin}2x)+({x}^{2})(2\\operatorname{cos}2x) \\\\  & =2x\\operatorname{sin}2x+2{x}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}2x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope $m$ at $x=\\frac{\\pi }{4}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m & ={(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x})|}_{x=\\pi /4} \\\\  & =2(\\frac{\\pi }{4})\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}(2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{4})+2{(\\frac{\\pi }{4})}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}(2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{4}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}(\\frac{\\pi }{2})+2\\cdot \\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{16}\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}(\\frac{\\pi }{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\cdot 1+\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{8}\\cdot 0 \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233787 :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "2bd5c98f-2174-5540-b7d6-172750f2ba2f" :question/math-academy-id "q-31355" :question/problem "Find ${f}^{'}(x)$ if $f(x)=\\frac{\\operatorname{cot}x}{{x}^{2}}.$" :question/worked-solution "We calculate the derivative using the quotient rule, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}{f}^{'}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{\\operatorname{cot}x}{{x}^{2}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{(-{csc}^{2}x)\\cdot {x}^{2}-\\operatorname{cot}x\\cdot (2x)}{{x}^{4}} \\\\  & =\\frac{-x{csc}^{2}x-2\\operatorname{cot}x}{{x}^{3}}\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233805 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/id #uuid "c8500907-8a98-5f06-a757-84bd2768233a" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186232568} {:answer-field/id #uuid "43fbe94e-b03e-5ff3-baba-4915744af8ee" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186232569}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "0dbc1b1f-4a2e-5ef0-8fc5-7941053eff59" :question/math-academy-id "q-316031" :question/problem "The displacement $s$ (in meters) relative to a fixed origin $O$ of a particle moving in a straight line along the $x$-axis is given by the equation $s(t)=\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}(t+1)+6}{{t}^{2}+1}$, where $t$ is the time, measured in seconds. Find the position ${s}_{0}$ and velocity ${v}_{0}$ of the particle at the initial moment of time $t=0\\,\\text{s}.$\n\n${s}_{0}=$\n{{field-1}}$\\text{m}\\,\\text{and}\\,$\n${v}_{0}=$\n{{field-2}}$\\text{m/s}$" :question/worked-solution "To calculate the initial position, we substitute $t=0$ it into the equation $s(t)=\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}(t+1)+6}{{t}^{2}+1}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{s}_{0} & =s(0) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}(0+1)+6}{{0}^{2}+1} \\\\  & =6\\,\\text{m}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate the velocity function by differentiating the position function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}s}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}(t+1)+6}{{t}^{2}+1}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\frac{1}{t+1}\\cdot ({t}^{2}+1)-(\\operatorname{ln}(t+1)+6)\\cdot 2t}{({t}^{2}+1)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{{t}^{2}+1-2t(t+1)(\\operatorname{ln}(t+1)+6)}{(t+1)({t}^{2}+1)^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo calculate the velocity at $t=0\\,\\text{s},$ we substitute $t=0$ into the velocity function above:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{0} & =v(0) \\\\  & =\\frac{{0}^{2}+1-2(0)(0+1)(\\operatorname{ln}(0+1)+6)}{(0+1)({0}^{2}+1)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{1\\cdot {1}^{2}} \\\\  & =1\\,\\text{m/s}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233810 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7f89b295-c0ea-51e0-baed-7b397b5dcf86" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1.21" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186232581}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7f89b295-c0ea-51e0-baed-7b397b5dcf86" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1.21" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186232581} :answer-field/id #uuid "a886217b-6842-59bb-806c-1abaff801bc1" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186232580}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "1592937d-0c9f-5407-8fea-82da11709905" :question/math-academy-id "q-319487" :question/problem "Find, **in radians**, the argument of $z=3+8\\text{i}.$ Round your final answer to **two decimal places**.\n\n**Hint**: The argument of a complex number is in the range $[0,2\\pi ).$ \n\n$\\operatorname{arg}(z)\\approx $\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Let's plot $z$ in the complex plane.\n\n![](images/fc/fc3602d0d057902e1a510485ae8f02746afd80ea3bb7c2c8efc40ccfaf7b3dd4.png)\n\nThe complex number $z$ lies in the first quadrant of the complex plane. Hence $0<\\operatorname{arg}(z)<\\frac{\\pi }{2}.$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{arg}(z)=arctan\\,(\\frac{8}{3})\\approx 1.21,\n$$\n\nrounded to $2$ decimal places."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233811 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b95c2ee5-2ec4-5420-8790-4297f324969f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2.52" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186232583}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b95c2ee5-2ec4-5420-8790-4297f324969f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2.52" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186232583} :answer-field/id #uuid "b5894526-c5ce-54bf-af3e-c52a4679eb4c" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186232582}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "ab9c463c-15d0-59d7-84a9-bb7f003f61d0" :question/math-academy-id "q-319495" :question/problem "Find, **in radians**, the argument of $z=-7+5\\text{i}.$ Round your final answer to **two decimal places**.\n\n**Hint**: The argument of a complex number is in the range $[0,2\\pi ).$ \n\n$\\operatorname{arg}(z)\\approx $\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Let's plot $z$ in the complex plane.\n\n![](images/86/86e7a97f9dcdec8a81d5362c79f15887da8ce13248c4758eafa49e960d8cf128.png)\n\nThe complex number $z$ lies in the second quadrant of the complex plane. Hence $\\frac{\\pi }{2}<\\operatorname{arg}(z)<\\pi .$ \n\nIf $\\alpha $ is the angle that $z$ makes with the negative real axis, then\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{tan}\\alpha  & =\\frac{5}{7} \\\\ \\alpha  & =\\operatorname{arctan}(\\frac{5}{7}) \\\\  & =0.620\\text{ }249\\text{ }…\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\theta =\\operatorname{arg}(z)=\\pi -0.620\\text{ }249\\text{ }…\\approx 2.52,\n$$\n\nrounded to $2$ decimal places."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233812 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0285aa91-2267-505a-9e1d-470bdb4e3618" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7\\pi }{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186232585}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0285aa91-2267-505a-9e1d-470bdb4e3618" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7\\pi }{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186232585} :answer-field/id #uuid "ca7f0a80-f55b-5dc3-ab7d-f8cf4328aca7" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186232584}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "247e6fd4-010c-5cfd-adfe-f21a9b3e2d4f" :question/math-academy-id "q-319554" :question/problem "Find, **in radians**, the **exact value** of the argument of $z=-6\\sqrt{3}-6\\text{i}.$\n\n**Hint**: The argument of a complex number is in the range $[0,2\\pi ).$ \n\n$\\operatorname{arg}(z)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Let's plot $z$ in the complex plane.\n\n![](images/ea/ea995ef688b12c8d58076fc832c9a5c735ff5933aff3ac6c347ae27850adbb2e.png)\n\nThe complex number $z$ lies in the third quadrant of the complex plane. Hence $\\pi <\\operatorname{arg}(z)<\\frac{3\\pi }{2}.$ \n\nIf $\\alpha $ is the angle that $z$ makes with the negative real axis, then\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{tan}\\alpha  & =\\frac{6}{6\\sqrt{3}} \\\\ \\alpha  & =arctan\\,(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\theta =\\operatorname{arg}(z)=\\pi +\\frac{\\pi }{6}=\\frac{7\\pi }{6}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233821 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a6e226fd-e173-5cad-9064-6119c8911894" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "45" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298282}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a6e226fd-e173-5cad-9064-6119c8911894" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "45" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298282} :answer-field/id #uuid "b7aef941-6c87-5479-b136-1350d6d0ff15" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186299258}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "226c9925-81e4-51c2-ba8a-4f634b0b9f69" :question/math-academy-id "q-320665" :question/problem "Expressed in degrees, $\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2})=${{field-1}}${}^{∘}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, note that the range of $\\theta =\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡x$ is given by\n\n$$\n-{90}^{∘}\\le \\theta \\le {90}^{∘}.\n$$\n\nNote that the angle $\\theta $ spans the first and fourth quadrants.\n\nSince $\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}$ is positive, our answer is an angle that lies in the first quadrant, where sine is positive.\n\n![](images/d9/d93c490adbb5cdbc0e9a09b60eea9b82ab93be8e871e334423abc17ddd62955e.png)\n\nFrom the values in the first quadrant, we see that\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡({45}^{∘})=\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2})={45}^{∘}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233822 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b94ac8fa-3afb-5182-af32-183f997a33ef" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186232607}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b94ac8fa-3afb-5182-af32-183f997a33ef" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186232607} :answer-field/id #uuid "45ab3752-6964-55c3-b952-057c4a607718" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186232606}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "dd08cdae-26ba-5621-9698-862239b9ebc9" :question/math-academy-id "q-320723" :question/problem "Expressed in radians, $\\operatorname{arctan}(\\sqrt{3})=${{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "The range of $\\theta =\\operatorname{arctan}x$ is given by:\n\n$$\n-\\frac{\\pi }{2}<\\theta <\\frac{\\pi }{2}\n$$\n\nNote that the angle $\\theta $ spans the first and fourth quadrants.\n\nSince $\\sqrt{3}$ is positive, our answer is an angle that lies in the first quadrant, where the tangent is positive.\n\nFrom the special values of the trigonometric functions, we know that \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{tan}(\\frac{\\pi }{3})=\\sqrt{3}.\n$$\n\nSince $\\frac{\\pi }{3}$ is an angle in the first quadrant that lies within the range of $arctan,$ we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{arctan}(\\sqrt{3})=\\frac{\\pi }{3}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233824 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "453880c3-1ae3-55e4-a4e9-b1cd442aeb05" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-120" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186232609}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "453880c3-1ae3-55e4-a4e9-b1cd442aeb05" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-120" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186232609} :answer-field/id #uuid "495a7f1c-94c5-5185-9c3e-b28de350339f" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186232608}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "ab5a32f4-676c-579d-8de1-7499420b539a" :question/math-academy-id "q-321138" :question/problem "Expressed in degrees, $4\\operatorname{arctan}(-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3})=${{field-1}}${}^{∘}.$" :question/worked-solution "The range of $\\theta =\\operatorname{arctan}x$ is given by\n\n$$\n-{90}^{∘}<\\theta <{90}^{∘}.\n$$\n\nNote that the angle $\\theta $ spans the first and fourth quadrants.\n\nSince $-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}$ is negative, our answer is an angle that lies in the fourth quadrant, where the tangent is negative.\n\nFrom the special values of the trigonometric functions and the oddness of the tangent function, we know that \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{tan}(-{30}^{∘})=-\\operatorname{tan}({30}^{∘})=-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}.\n$$\n\nSince $-{30}^{∘}$ lies within the range of $arctan,$ we conclude that\n\n$$\narctan\\,(-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3})=-{30}^{∘}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we get\n\n$$\n4arctan\\,(-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3})=4\\cdot (-{30}^{∘})={-120}^{∘}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233834 :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "ca47a5ef-4902-5764-841f-f4a92c465140" :question/math-academy-id "q-32667" :question/problem "If $x=2{y}^{2}+7y-\\frac{3}{y},$ then $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=$" :question/worked-solution "First, we differentiate $x(y)$ with respect to $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}(2{y}^{2}+7y-\\frac{3}{y}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}(2{y}^{2}+7y-3{y}^{-1}) \\\\  & =2\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}({y}^{2})+7\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}(y)-3\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}({y}^{-1}) \\\\  & =4y+7+3{y}^{-2} \\\\  & =4y+7+\\frac{3}{{y}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{4{y}^{3}+7{y}^{2}+3}{{y}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, taking the reciprocal of $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}={(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y})}^{-1}=\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4{y}^{3}+7{y}^{2}+3}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233835 :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "0dc75a02-252b-57f2-b5e0-2f28340d9b40" :question/math-academy-id "q-32711" :question/problem "Given a curve $x=6{y}^{2}+2y-5,$ what is the equation of the tangent to the curve at the point $(3,1)?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we differentiate $x(y)$ with respect to $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}(6{y}^{2}+2y-5) \\\\  & =6\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}({y}^{2})+2\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}(y)-5\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}(1) \\\\  & =6\\cdot (2y)+2(1)+0 \\\\  & =12y+2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, taking the reciprocal of $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}={(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y})}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{12y+2}.\n$$\n\nThe slope of the tangent to the curve at the point $(3,1)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m & =\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}{|}_{(x,y)=(3,1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{12\\cdot 1+2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{14}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the point-slope formula for a tangent line, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{y}_{1} & =m(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ y-1 & =\\frac{1}{14}(x-3) \\\\ y & =\\frac{x}{14}-\\frac{3}{14}+1 \\\\ y & =\\frac{x}{14}+\\frac{11}{14}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233843 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "85e64727-b53e-59d6-8900-bae452d26930" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186232685}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "85e64727-b53e-59d6-8900-bae452d26930" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186232685} :answer-field/id #uuid "df56f7a5-e11b-5412-88c9-326f01d4dde9" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186232684} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0fd09e40-2b5b-50c9-a2dc-8cff50b81588" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186232687}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0fd09e40-2b5b-50c9-a2dc-8cff50b81588" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186232687} :answer-field/id #uuid "7a81aa69-8076-5dde-b419-ce6bacc29168" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186232686}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "2aa915f6-8e31-5601-8256-7110f8cdfa0f" :question/math-academy-id "q-329179" :question/problem "Express $\\operatorname{sin}(x+{240}^{∘})$ in terms of $\\operatorname{sin}x$ and $\\operatorname{cos}x.$\n\n$\\operatorname{sin}(x+{240}^{∘})=$\n{{field-1}}$\\operatorname{sin}x\\,-$\n{{field-2}}$\\operatorname{cos}x$" :question/worked-solution "Recall the sum formula for sine:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}(u+v)=\\operatorname{sin}u\\operatorname{cos}v+\\operatorname{cos}u\\operatorname{sin}v\n$$\n\nApplying the sum formula for sine with $u=x$ and $v={240}^{∘},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}(x+{240}^{∘}) & =\\operatorname{sin}x\\operatorname{cos}{240}^{∘}+\\operatorname{cos}x\\operatorname{sin}{240}^{∘} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}x\\cdot (-\\frac{1}{2})+\\operatorname{cos}x\\cdot (-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}\\,\\operatorname{sin}x-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\,\\operatorname{cos}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233844 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "395be8dd-8a08-50b0-b651-f5463e741316" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186232689}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "395be8dd-8a08-50b0-b651-f5463e741316" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186232689} :answer-field/id #uuid "d9940104-7cb5-539d-bd4b-4fc0cdbc7291" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186232688} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2b8fe09a-d274-52e6-8978-1245